Recall that $p$ is a prime with $p \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$. Note that this implies $p \equiv 3 \pmod{4}$, so $-1$ is not a quadratic residue modulo $p$. The $p \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ condition implies that $p > 3$. This ensures that $ rac{p-1}{2} 
ot\equiv \pm 1 \pmod{p}$. We use this fact repeatedly. Partition $V(\overline{G})$ into the following connected sets of size at most $2$:  egin{itemize} \item Type 1 sets: these are singleton sets of the form $\{ (i, 0) \}$ for $i \in \mathbb{F}_p \setminus \{ rac{p-1}{2}, p-1\}$. \item A Type 2 set: a single $2$-element set: $\{ ( rac{p-1}{2} , 0), (p-1, 0) \}$. \item Type 3 sets: these are subsets $\{ (j, i), (j, p-1-i) \}$ for $j \in \mathbb{F}_p$ and $i \in \{ 1, \dots,  rac{p-1}{2} - 1 \}$. \item Type 4 sets: these are the subsets $\{ (j,  rac{p-1}{2} ), (j, p-1) \}$ for $j \in \mathbb{F}_p$. \end{itemize}  egin{lem} Suppose there is a vertex $(a,b) \in V(\overline{G})$ that is not adjacent to a Type $3$ set $\{ (j, i), (j, p-1-i) \}$ in $\overline{G}$ for some $j \in \mathbb{F}_p$ and $i \in \{ 1, \dots,  rac{p-1}{2} - 1 \}$. Then $b \equiv  rac{p-1}{2} \pmod{p}$. \end{lem}  egin{proof} Since $(a,b)(j,i) \in E(G)$ and $(a,b)(j, p-1-i) \in E(G)$, we have $(a-j, b-i), (a-j, b-p+1+i) \in S$. This implies $a-j 
eq 0$, and by definition of $S$, we have $b-i \equiv \pm (b+i+1) \pmod{p}$. If the sign is positive, then $2i \equiv -1 \pmod{p}$, but this does not occur because $i \in \{ 1, \dots,  rac{p-1}{2} - 1 \}$. If the sign is negative, then $b-i \equiv -(b+i+1) \pmod{p}$. Thus $2b \equiv -1 \pmod{p}$, so $b \equiv  rac{p-1}{2} \pmod{p}$. \end{proof} We check that the sets are pairwise adjacent in $\overline{G}$.  egin{itemize} \item Two Type $1$ sets are adjacent: if $(a,0)$ is not adjacent to $(b,0)$ for distinct $a, b \in \mathbb{F}_p \setminus \{ rac{p-1}{2}, p -1\}$, then $(b-a, 0) \in S$, but this contradicts \cref{obs:cay}. \item Two Type $2$ sets are adjacent: vacuously true as there is only one Type $2$ set. \item Two Type $3$ sets are adjacent: take a vertex $(a,b)$ in a Type $3$ set. Since $b 
ot\equiv  rac{p-1}{2} \pmod{p}$, \cref{lem:minorConnection} implies $(a,b)$ is adjacent to all Type $3$ sets. \item Two Type $4$ sets are adjacent: straightforward by \cref{obs:cay}. \item A Type $1$ set and a Type $2$ set are adjacent: straightforward by \cref{obs:cay}. \item A Type $1$ set and a Type $3$ set are adjacent: by \cref{lem:minorConnection}, any vertex $(a,b)$ not adjacent to a Type $3$ set has $b \equiv  rac{p-1}{2} \pmod{p}$. Therefore $(a,b)$ is not in a Type $1$ set. \item A Type $1$ set and a Type $4$ set are adjacent: if there is $i \in \mathbb{F}_p \setminus \{ rac{p-1}{2}, p -1\}$ and $j \in \mathbb{F}_p$ such that $(i,0)$ is not adjacent to $(j,  rac{p-1}{2})$ and $(j, p-1)$, then $(j-i,  rac{p-1}{2}), (j-i, p-1) \in S$. By \cref{obs:cay}, $ rac{p-1}{2} \equiv \pm (p-1) \equiv \pm 1 \pmod{p}$. This is a contradiction since $p > 3$. \item A Type $2$ set and a Type $3$ set are adjacent: by \cref{lem:minorConnection}, any vertex $(a,b)$ not adjacent to a Type $3$ set has $b \equiv  rac{p-1}{2} \pmod{p}$, implying that $(a,b)$ is not in a Type $2$ set. \item A Type $2$ set and a Type $4$ set are adjacent: consider the vertex $(j, p-1)$ in a Type $4$ set for some $j \in \mathbb{F}_p$. If $(j, p-1)$ is not adjacent to $( rac{p-1}{2} , 0)$ and $(p-1, 0)$ in $\overline{G}$, then $(j -  rac{p-1}{2} , p-1) \in S$ and $(j - (p-1), p-1) \in S$. As $p \equiv 3 \pmod{4}$, $-1$ is not a quadratic residue mod $p$, which implies that $(j -  rac{p-1}{2} , p-1)$ is of the form $(a, -a^2)$ for some $a \in \mathbb{F}_p$, and $(j - (p-1), p-1)$ is of the form $(b, -b^2)$ for some $b \in \mathbb{F}_p$. Hence, $(j-  rac{p-1}{2})^2 \equiv (j - (p-1))^2 \equiv -(p-1) \equiv 1 \pmod{p}$. Thus, $j -  rac{p-1}{2}$ and $j - (p-1)$ are roots of the polynomial $x^2 - 1 \equiv 0 \pmod{p}$. Therefore, $j -  rac{p-1}{2}, j - (p-1) \in \{1, -1\}$. Since $p > 3$, $j -  rac{p-1}{2} 
ot\equiv j - (p-1) \pmod{p}$. Consider two cases. If $j -  rac{p-1}{2} \equiv 1 \pmod{p}$ and $j - (p-1) \equiv -1 \pmod{p}$, then the latter equation implies that $j \equiv p - 2 \pmod{p}$. The former equation implies that $j \equiv 1 +  rac{p-1}{2} \pmod{p}$. This implies that $p - 2 \equiv 1 +  rac{p-1}{2} \pmod{p}$, and thus $5 \equiv 0 \pmod{p}$, a contradiction. If $j -  rac{p-1}{2} \equiv -1 \pmod{p}$ and $j - (p-1) \equiv 1 \pmod{p}$, then the latter equation implies that $j \equiv 0 \pmod{p}$. Setting $j \equiv 0$ in the first equation implies that $ rac{p-1}{2} \equiv 1 \pmod{p}$, which is a contradiction since $p > 3$. \item A Type $3$ set and a Type $4$ set are adjacent: by \cref{lem:minorConnection}, any vertex $(a,b)$ not adjacent to a Type $3$ set has $b \equiv  rac{p-1}{2} \pmod{p}$. Hence a Type $4$ set contains a vertex adjacent to the Type $3$ set. \end{itemize} The sets form a $K_{(p^2 + p - 2) / 2}$-model of $\overline{G}$, which contains a $K_{(p^2 + 1) / 2}$-model. By \cref{lem:chromatic_ebhard}, $\overline{G}$ satisfies 
ameref{conj:shc_chi}.
